  "strategy": "1. Approach and rationale\nThe central idea is to minimize the AC1 upper bound by iteratively optimizing the sequence coefficients using a hybrid optimization strategy that combines gradient-based and constraint-aware gradient-free methods. Unlike the previous LP-based approach, this method uses automatic differentiation to compute accurate gradient or subgradient for the objective function and constraint functions, enabling direct gradient-based descent on the optimization landscape. The rationale is that precise gradient calculations can more efficiently navigate through the optimization space, especially near the global optimum where the objective landscape is smoother. This approach systematically balances exploration and exploitation using dual-phase optimization:\n- A strong local descent phase with gradient-based updates\n- A global exploration phase using constraint-handling variants of gradient-free non-dominated sorting genetic algorithm (NSGA-II) - s\u5169 variants to escape local optima\n\nThe main limitation is that gradient calculations may be computationally expensive, but this cost is justified by the faster convergence compared to hit-and-trial/brute-force methods. The method leverages modern optimization libraries to handle technical aspects and preserves the core structure of the problem\u2014non-negative coefficients and coefficient cap\u2014but more effectively addresses the trade-offs between maximizing the sequence energy and minimizing peak autocorrelation.\n\n2. Mathematical specification\nRepresentation: Input sequence coefficients (floats) of length n, initialized from parent if available or randomly generated. \nVariables: $a_i \\in \\mathbb{R}^+$ for each $i \\in [0, n-1}$, $0 \\leq a_i \\leq 1000$, $\\sum a_i \\geq 0.01$\nObjective: Strictly minimize $f(a) = 2n \\cdot \\max(\\operatorname{convolve}(a,a)) / (\\sum a)^2$ (model: linear convolution, mode='full')\nConstraints: Non-negativity $a_i \\geq 0$, coefficient cap $a_i \\leq 1000$, and initializer normalization $\\sum a_i \\geq 0.01$\n\nAlgorithmic formulation:\n- **Initialization**: Use parent sequence or generate random initialization\n- **Local Descent (Gradient-based)**:\n   - Formulate constrained optimization via Lagrange multipliers\n   - Compute gradients using automatic differentiation\n   - Use projected gradient descent with adaptive learning rate\n   - Handle active constraints (tight constraints) via complementarity conditions\n- **Global Exploration (NSGA-II variants)**:\n   - Hybrid mutation combining select gradient components and local perturbations\n   - Maintain non-dominated archive based on two objectives:\n        Primary: $f(a)$ value\n        Secondary: Normalized sequence energy $\\sum(a^2)$\n   - Use $\\epsilon$-constraints to better articulate tradeoffs\n\nTermination conditions:\n- Time budget 1300s across named phases\n- Relative improvement drop $< 0.0001\\%$\n- Constraint tightness $< 1e-6$\n\n3. Implementation procedure\n- **Phase 1: Parsing and initialization**\n   - Accept parent height_sequence_1(320) or generate random initializations\n   - Use optimize_sequence_1 from provided utilities if available\n   - Desired length: if parent exists: 320, else start from 64 for interpolation to 320\n   - Scipy optimize.minimize with COBYLA method for constrained optimization\n\n- **Phase 2: Local descent**\n   - Compute objective gradient via autograd.ProdGrad (automatic differentiation)\n   - Objective function (score) must return relative improvement\n   - Gradient calculations for convolution and normalization are blocked (use finite differences if necessary)\n   - projected gradient update:\\begin{aligned}\n     a_{t+1} & = \\text{clip}\\left( a_t - \\alpha \\nabla f(a_t), L, U \\right) \\\\\n     & \\text{L=0, U=min recognition for parent's max+10\\%}\n   \\end{aligned}\n   - Polynomial decay schedule for learning rate:\n     $\\alpha_t = \\alpha_0 \\cdot (1 - t/T)^{power}$\n\n- **Phase 3: Global search (NSGA-II-like)**\n   - Formulate multi-objective problem:\n     $\\min f(a) \\qquad \\operatorname{\\&} \\min (\\text{energy penalty})$\n     $\\text{s.t. non-negativity and cap}$\n   - Use optmulti library implementation of NSGA-II\n   - Custom variation operator leveraging gradient information: \"gradient-informed crossover\"\n   - Selection pressure designed toward diversity in exploration\n\n- **Phase 4: Repair/Refinement**\n   - For NSGA-II offsprings, repair infeasible candidates by projection and renormalization\n   - For gradient-based, ensure non-negativity constraints are enforced explicitly\n   - Capitalize on the provided evaluate_sequence function for acceptance\n\n4. Search choices and useful variations\n- **Default Parameters**:\n   - Learning rate: $\\alpha_0 = 0.1$ (local), $\\alpha_0 = 0.05$ (global)\n   - Line search patience values: $5$\n   - NSGA-II mutation rate: $0.2$, crossover rate: $0.8$\n- **Detection Heuristics**:\n   - 50% reduction in search budget after 3 degradation cycles\n   - Sample gradient-free variants if Constraints naturally bind too frequently\n   - Interpolate between current winner and highest-ranked diverse candidate to revive local optima\n\n   - **Search Space Partitioning**:\n     - Strategy Exhibition: Local/steepest ascent vs Global/nSGA's harmony\n     - It \"Fractional\u062a\u062c\u0646\u0628 exploration: keep top p% of front and explore around them\n\n   - **Defined because of failure to converge with convex or standard anneals** but shrugged off as optional\n\n5. Compute budget and robustness\n- **Core Optimization Phases**:\n   - Tuple Programming: Multi-threaded if possible (only 1 CPU)\n   - Time Apportionment per constraint call\n- **Explicit Deadline Handling**:\n   - Estimate LP cost per parameter by available budget\n   - Parallel processing of subsetted evaluation batches\n   - Use simulate_expensive_constraint to warmup/provide early scores\n\n6. Validation and return contract\n- All best candidates evaluated via provided evaluate_sequence\n- Maintain best since start of run (not session)\n- First valid sequence fallback: Uniform distribution, all capped to 1000, normalized to sum 1.0\n- Score comparison: The AC1 bound, $\\frac{2n \\max(\\operatorname{convolve}(a,a,\\text{mode}=\\text{full}))}{(\\sum a)^2}$\n- Acceptance only if finite score and lower than best so far\n- For termination, if score > previous_best*1.0001, revert last best found\n\nSo, the strategy systematically improves through:\n- Phase 1: Reliable initialization near parent's vicinity\n- Phase 2: Local optimization with intensity\n- Phase 3: Unbiased, population-based search with exploitation balance\n- Phase 4: Broad repair but sequencing final output through score-evaluated acceptance list]",
